{
  "action": "tool",
  "tool": "retrieve_history",
  "args": {
    "research_concepts": [
      "lineup structural roles",
      "organizer shaper terminal roles",
      "lineup composition variation",
      "player talent measurement",
      "separability of role coverage from talent"
    ],
    "methodological_questions": [
      "defining player roles from play-by-play data",
      "measuring lineup coverage",
      "controlling for talent in lineup analysis",
      "demonstrating separability of lineup structure effects"
    ],
    "query": "lineup structural roles organizer shaper terminal talent control separability within-team variation"
  }
}

TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "lineup structural roles",
    "organizer shaper terminal roles",
    "lineup composition variation",
    "player talent measurement",
    "separability of role coverage from talent"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "defining player roles from play-by-play data",
    "measuring lineup coverage",
    "controlling for talent in lineup analysis",
    "demonstrating separability of lineup structure effects"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No historical evidence was available for this specific research question."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No historical evidence was available to establish any findings or limitations for this specific research question."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The research question requires a robust methodology to define and assign 'organizer', 'shaper', and 'terminal' roles to players based on their actions in play-by-play data. This classification must then be aggregated to the lineup level to determine 'lineup coverage'.",
    "The core challenge lies in disentangling the effect of this structural role coverage from the aggregate talent and individual player counts within a lineup. This necessitates careful selection and application of control variables for player talent and compositional attributes.",
    "The phrase 'talent control behaves' implies an expectation that talent controls will operate as anticipated and that the role coverage effects will remain significant and interpretable after accounting for talent.",
    "The available data (play-by-play, derived play-type possessions) is suitable for developing player role assignments and analyzing lineup compositions, offering granular detail necessary for this type of structural analysis."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The precise definitions and operationalization of 'organizer', 'shaper', and 'terminal' roles from raw play-by-play data are not specified and will require significant methodological development.",
    "The best method for quantifying 'player talent' and 'counts' for control purposes (e.g., which advanced metrics, how to aggregate them to a lineup level) is not predetermined.",
    "The specific functional form for modeling the relationship between lineup coverage, talent, and outcomes, while accounting for within-team variation, is yet to be determined.",
    "The expected magnitude of the effect of structural role coverage, independent of talent, is unknown."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was retrieved, so there was no external information to change the decision. The assessment is based solely on the research question, available data, and general methodological principles."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.8,
    "blocking_constraints": []
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No historical evidence was available for this specific research question, indicating it has not been directly addressed by prior work within this project."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Defining 'organizer, shaper, terminal' roles from play-by-play data is complex and subjective. The reliability and validity of these role assignments will be critical and could vary across players, teams, and seasons."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Separating the effect of structural role coverage from the aggregate talent level and specific player attributes ('counts') within a lineup is a significant confounding risk. High-talent players often naturally fill multiple roles, making it difficult to isolate the 'coverage' effect itself."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Specific combinations of lineup structural coverage (e.g., having exactly one of each role type, or certain players filling those roles) might be rare, especially within a single team, leading to insufficient data for robust analysis of within-team variation."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "While overall possessions are high, the number of distinct lineup-coverage configurations *within* a single team across seasons, especially after controlling for talent, might be limited, potentially leading to underpowered tests for within-team effects."
    },
    {
      "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
      "text": "If 'talent control behaves' is interpreted as an outcome, it's a qualitative assessment. If it's about the statistical significance or direction of talent coefficients, it's an expected part of the model but not a direct hypothesis test on a derived label."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.2,
    "rejected": 0.2,
    "untestable": 0.1,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.4
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The causal effect of having a lineup with at least one player classified as an 'organizer', one as a 'shaper', and one as a 'terminal' on offensive and defensive efficiency, after controlling for individual player talent and other lineup compositional attributes, observed across all 90 team-seasons.",
    "unit": "Lineup-possession. Each unique lineup on the court for each possession will be the unit of analysis.",
    "sample": "All 420,993 possessions from NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25 regular seasons and playoffs, and NBA 2025-26 regular season and playoffs (complete through 2026-06-21). This includes 90 team-seasons.",
    "power": "Power will be assessed by simulating data based on plausible effect sizes and observed variance. Given the large number of possessions, power is likely sufficient for detecting moderate effects, but may be limited for rare lineup compositions or small within-team effects. A minimum number of possessions per lineup-type-team combination will be required (e.g., 50 possessions) to be included in the analysis.",
    "controls": "Individual player talent will be controlled using established advanced metrics (e.g., a composite score of per-minute efficiency, usage, and impact metrics like RAPTOR or EPM) for each player in the lineup, aggregated (e.g., sum, average) to the lineup level. Additional controls will include: number of primary ball-handlers, number of primary scorers, positional composition (guards, forwards, centers), and opponent strength. Team fixed effects and season fixed effects will be included to account for unobserved team-level and season-level variations.",
    "falsification": "A falsification test will involve permuting the assigned structural roles within teams for players with similar statistical profiles, and verifying that the observed effects diminish or disappear. Another test will involve analyzing the predictive power of the role coverage metric in a separate, unseen portion of the data (e.g., 2025-26 playoffs if not used for training).",
    "multiplicity": "Given that the question involves a single primary estimand (the effect of a specific lineup coverage), multiplicity correction will not be the primary concern. However, if multiple specifications of role definitions or talent controls are explored, Bonferroni or False Discovery Rate adjustments will be applied to p-values.",
    "decision_rule": "The research question will be considered 'supported' if the estimated coefficient for the target lineup coverage variable (>=1 organizer, shaper, terminal) is statistically significant (p < 0.05) in the hypothesized direction (e.g., positive for offensive efficiency) after controlling for talent and other confounders, and if the talent controls themselves exhibit expected and significant relationships with the outcome.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, 2025-26 regular season and playoffs data, complete as of 2026-07-13."
  }
}

